From: szabo@netcom.com (Nick Szabo)
Date: Tue, 29 Nov 1994 01:39:25 -0800 (PST)
Subject: [#94-11-480] COMPUTERS: the persistence of C

Ray Cromwell: 
>   Actually, the next generation of games systems are already
> moving to operating systems and high level language. 

And as you go on to point out, in each case the programming language
is C[++].  This is because C allows one to program the hardware
both portably _and_ efficiently at the same time, a crucial feature 
missing from other major high level languages because their academic 
designers dismiss the importance of making software efficient, or because
the languages are designed for writing customized rather than widely
used software.  The triumph of C into the games arena, without the
benefit of any of the existing base of source code that strengthens
the future of C in systems and mass market applications, again make 
my point that use of C is expanding, not contracting, and it will 
hardly be a minor player 15 years from now.
 
>   Look at all the new PDAs, they are all based on scripting languages.
> (NewtonScript, Telescript). 

Perhaps not coincidentally, PDAs have also flopped.  Perhaps, unlike
the demo groupies who sold and were sold on these kind of PDAs, real 
users and programmers are not into the gratuitous inefficiencies and 
decorations of the scripting languages and GUIs.

