From: Phil Goetz <goetz@cs.Buffalo.EDU>
Date: Tue, 29 Nov 1994 10:40:52 -0500
Subject: [#94-11-482] Population, Simon Sez, and Environment

> My impression from driving accross Pennsylvania is that you should have seen
>about 50% of the land covered by forest unless the plane detours to the east.
>Your "except on hillsides" could refer to half the land, so I'm not sure
>whether your "NO FOREST" means much.

By hillsides I mean the Appalachians.

If you see the ground from the air, you will see that often the freeway
is surrounded by a zone of forest up to a quarter mile wide on each side,
presumably because people don't like to live next to the freeway.
That is the case in western New York.

Pennsylvania does have a lot of forest, as I think I mentioned in my post,
but it wasn't in my flight path.  (In fact, I drove thru PA yesterday, on
Route 15, and saw hunters by the roadside in their orange parkas.  The
first day of hunting season is effectively a state holiday in parts of PA.)

In Simon's world, there would be no room for such forests.  He speaks only
of the need for "recreational areas", presumably like Central Park.  It
seems clear from his writing that he would like the whole world to be more
like Hong Kong, Taiwain, or Holland than like Pennsylvania.  He reminds me
of a visitor from mainland China who asked me, "Why do you have all these
forests? Why don't you cut them down?"

>Gee, last I heard environmentalists were critisizing many high fat foods
>(particulary beef) because they take more land per calorie to produce.
>What is a calorie-free food? Nutrasweet? It's not obvious that this takes
>more land per calorie to produce than any other food high in amino acids.
>All that is changing here is the taste to calorie ratio. I think any trend
>to produce less calories per acre is a figment of your imagination.

I have no evidence of such a trend.  I'm just
saying it could happen.  Since most people like to eat,
but don't like to be fat, we can develop foods that taste great AND are
less filling.  So the food "requirements" of the US and Europe could
increase a great deal.  Perhaps this food will be produced in vats
instead of in fields.  That would be great.

This is an instance of a general principle which I have a hard time
getting across --- technology enables individuals to consume more.

My concern is not really with the food supply, but with land area.

Phil goetz@cs.buffalo.edu

