From: Phil Goetz <goetz@cs.Buffalo.EDU>
Date: Tue, 29 Nov 1994 11:55:40 -0500
Subject: [#94-11-485] Population, Simon Sez, and Environment

>- Why is it that the problems of environment can be solved by
>government force, by intimidation, by threats, but ...
>can't be solved by prices? If land is so valuable, why doesn't
>it cost more? 
>
>This is, of course, academic, because prices will always prevail,
>even if force triumphs temporararily.          
>
>How will the problem of overpopulation be solved:
>- I will pay for my space.

Forests are communal.  The amount of land I could buy by myself would
be miniscule.

That could be overcome by groups that time-share large plots of land.
But the basic problem is the supposition that there is an absolute market
value, and that it is the "correct" value.

The market value of a resource is set in part by governmental policies,
which we set according to societal values.  There is no "true market value"
unless there is no government, and I think we need government.  Not
necessarily a central government, but something to keep everybody from
externalizing their costs.

There are values prior to market values -- moral values.  We use them to
decide what our government is supposed to do -- what practices it will
discourage/encourage.  If not, why even discuss these issues?
Why have a government?

Phil goetz@cs.buffalo.edu

